[{:db/id 17592186105823 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b04f202f-7340-5122-b2d5-59ca0d357302"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105828 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-098ae999-41d1-5d21-93d9-672720b112c8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b04f202f-7340-5122-b2d5-59ca0d357302" :question/id #uuid "b04f202f-7340-5122-b2d5-59ca0d357302" :question/math-academy-id "q-129104" :question/problem "Two fair  four-sided dice are rolled and $X$ is the absolute difference of their scores. The probability mass function $f(x)$ of $X$ is shown in the table below. What is $\\text{Var}[X]?$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{2}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{2}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |" :question/worked-solution "The variance of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{2}{8})+1(\\frac{3}{8})+2(\\frac{2}{8})+3(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the variance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{(x-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & ={(0-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{(1-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{(2-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{(3-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{5}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{(-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{(\\frac{7}{4})}^{2}\\cdot f(3) \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{16})(\\frac{2}{8})+(\\frac{1}{16})(\\frac{3}{8})+(\\frac{9}{16})(\\frac{2}{8})+(\\frac{49}{16})(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-735318e8-b065-526f-8046-9b9ea9da16fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-735318e8-b065-526f-8046-9b9ea9da16fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "735318e8-b065-526f-8046-9b9ea9da16fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" :answer-field/choices ["answer-637b3062-8acc-5b54-9040-15b955fa09f3" "answer-fef72be8-42ee-5d66-9f10-03ce259c9db4" "answer-f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" "answer-26cb8fc2-022b-568c-b41a-7bbf10255e99" "answer-96670029-c0b7-54da-8a19-99c6872c02f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-637b3062-8acc-5b54-9040-15b955fa09f3" :answer/id #uuid "637b3062-8acc-5b54-9040-15b955fa09f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef72be8-42ee-5d66-9f10-03ce259c9db4" :answer/id #uuid "fef72be8-42ee-5d66-9f10-03ce259c9db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" :answer/id #uuid "f3700714-cf33-5fb7-9887-c4e1c1ca2d27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26cb8fc2-022b-568c-b41a-7bbf10255e99" :answer/id #uuid "26cb8fc2-022b-568c-b41a-7bbf10255e99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96670029-c0b7-54da-8a19-99c6872c02f4" :answer/id #uuid "96670029-c0b7-54da-8a19-99c6872c02f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-098ae999-41d1-5d21-93d9-672720b112c8" :question/id #uuid "098ae999-41d1-5d21-93d9-672720b112c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-84199" :question/problem "Let $X$ be the outcome of rolling an unfair  six-sided die that follows the probability mass function $f(x)$ shown in the table below. What is $\\text{Var}[X]?$\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+2(\\frac{3}{8})+3(\\frac{1}{4})+4(\\frac{1}{8})+5(\\frac{1}{16})+6(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{8})+4(\\frac{3}{8})+9(\\frac{1}{4})+16(\\frac{1}{8})+25(\\frac{1}{16})+36(\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}-{(\\frac{45}{16})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{155}{16}-\\frac{2025}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{455}{256}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9520dcff-aa35-56e0-9ddc-bd7600942c23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9520dcff-aa35-56e0-9ddc-bd7600942c23" :answer-field/id #uuid "9520dcff-aa35-56e0-9ddc-bd7600942c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515" :answer-field/choices ["answer-585f608f-5a28-539a-9ae1-19a60685c3c2" "answer-f963b964-0ba8-5f1c-b778-714106f60c39" "answer-d098068f-0f1e-5a9a-b34b-77d4043a3eac" "answer-dc6e32fe-4a49-5537-a523-6b3d99454374" "answer-5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-585f608f-5a28-539a-9ae1-19a60685c3c2" :answer/id #uuid "585f608f-5a28-539a-9ae1-19a60685c3c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{155}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f963b964-0ba8-5f1c-b778-714106f60c39" :answer/id #uuid "f963b964-0ba8-5f1c-b778-714106f60c39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{255}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d098068f-0f1e-5a9a-b34b-77d4043a3eac" :answer/id #uuid "d098068f-0f1e-5a9a-b34b-77d4043a3eac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{315}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc6e32fe-4a49-5537-a523-6b3d99454374" :answer/id #uuid "dc6e32fe-4a49-5537-a523-6b3d99454374" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{415}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515" :answer/id #uuid "5782b94b-8258-5e43-85df-a8d0c1327515" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{455}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107104 :tutorial/content "The **variance** of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} (x - \\textrm E[X])^2 \\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nIn other words, the variance is the expected value of the squared difference between $X$ and the expected value of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ]\n$$\n\nThe variance quantifies how \"spread out\" a probability distribution is. The expected value $\\textrm E[X]$ can be interpreted as the \"center\" of a probability distribution, and the squared difference $(X - \\textrm E[X])^2$ represents the squared distance between $X$ and the center. So, the variance quantifies how far the possible values of $X$ are from the center, on average.\n\nIn general,\n\n- a probability distribution that is *spread out* over many possible values will have a *high* variance, while\n\n- a probability distribution that is *concentrated* (or *clumped*) near a single value will have a *low* variance." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107106 :tutorial/content "Using the properties of the expected value of a discrete random variable $X,$ we can arrive at the following alternative formula for the variance of $X{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ X^2 ] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\nAs we will see in the following example, using this formula often speeds up the process of computing the variance." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107108 :tutorial/content "The **standard deviation** of a random variable $X$ is defined as the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{Var}[X] }\n$$\n\nAs we will see in the future, the standard deviation is often used when we observe a random variable and wish to quantify how \"surprising\" a particular observation was.\n\nIn those contexts, we often compute how many standard deviations the observed value was away from the expected value: the further away, the more surprising the observation.\n\nFor now, though, let's get some practice computing the standard deviation of a random variable." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107122 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following formula for the variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nTo begin, recall that the variance of a random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ (X - \\textrm E[X])^2 ].\n$$\n\nExpanding out the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =\\textrm E [ X^2 - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X + \\textrm E[X]^2 ] .\n$$\n\nThen, distributing the expectation over the sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [ X^2 ] + \\textrm E[ - 2\\textrm E[X] \\cdot X ] + \\textrm E[ \\textrm E[X]^2 ] .\n$$\n\nNow, we make the following simplifications:\n\n- Since $\\textrm E[aX] = a \\textrm E[X]$ for any constant $a,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[{\\color{blue}-2 \\textrm E[X]} \\cdot X] = {\\color{blue}- 2\\textrm E[X]} \\cdot \\textrm E[ X ] = - 2\\textrm E[X]^2.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\textrm{E}[X]$ is a constant, and $\\textrm E[a] = a$ for any constant $a,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[ {\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2} ] = {\\color{blue}\\textrm E[X]^2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+\\text{E}[-2\\text{E}[X]\\cdot X]+\\text{E}[E[X]^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-2\\text{E}[X]^{2}+\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106011 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
